  "response": "When a substation transformer fails and the resulting outage becomes a liability dispute, forensic engineers must decide between two competing physical narratives, each favouring a different party. In the case examined here, the utility argued that the failure originated in an internal insulation breakdown, a defect it attributed to the unit as manufactured and supplied. The manufacturer, by contrast, argued that the failure was caused by an external lightning surge, an event outside its control and outside the scope of its warranty obligations. Resolving such a dispute means reconstructing the electrical and physical sequence of the fault from records that neither party can easily manipulate.\n\nBecause the manufacturer advanced the lightning explanation, the utility's insurer retained an independent forensic engineering firm to investigate the failure. That engagement came after the moment at which lightning detection network data recorded a strike within two kilometers of the substation at the time of the fault. The investigators then turned to the protective equipment. The surge arrester counters at the substation registered an operation during the storm. The arrester record forces a binary question that the investigators could not leave open: either the surge arrester on the faulted phase was in working order at the time of the strike, or the surge arrester on the faulted phase was inoperative at the time of the strike, and exactly one of these conditions held. By contrast with the activity logged on those counters, the substation had no surge arresters installed on the transformer's high-voltage terminals, so the protection scheme at the terminals themselves had to be evaluated separately from the station-level devices.\n\nChemical evidence from inside the tank supplements the electrical record. Dissolved gas analysis of the transformer oil showed elevated acetylene levels after the failure. Acetylene is the classic marker of high-energy discharge, yet in this investigation the elevated acetylene indicates that dissolved gas analysis cannot distinguish arcing faults from thermal faults, because the gas signature appears within a mixture that a severe overheating event would also generate. Engineers therefore treat the oil chemistry as corroborating rather than decisive and look to direct physical examination of the core and coil assembly to establish where the breakdown began.\n\nTeardown inspection supplies that physical record. When the windings are unstacked and the paper and pressboard are tested for dielectric strength and water content, two explanations for the collapse of the insulation system come into view, and at least one of them holds, perhaps both: the winding insulation had degraded from moisture ingress, or the lightning surge exceeded the transformer's basic insulation level. Either mechanism alone can produce a dielectric failure, and the two can act together, with a weakened insulation system yielding to an impulse it would otherwise have withstood. Although the lightning surge exceeded the transformer's basic insulation level, the fault initiated at the high-voltage bushing lead. This restates in brief the more precise finding that the point of initial dielectric breakdown was the lead connected to the high-voltage bushing, not a location distributed along the line-end turns where an incoming impulse would normally concentrate its stress.\n\nThe location of initiation settles the tension between the surge magnitude and the site of failure. The independent forensic report concluded that internal insulation breakdown was the root cause of the transformer failure, and that conclusion confirms the bushing lead as the point of initial dielectric breakdown. A surge exceeding the basic insulation level establishes only that the transformer was stressed; it does not establish that the stress determined where and how the insulation gave way. By tracing the origin to an internal component, the report allocated causation to the condition of the insulation rather than to the external event. This is the general method forensic engineers apply in such disputes: they correlate lightning network timing, arrester counter data, oil chemistry and teardown findings, and they assign the root cause to whichever mechanism accounts for the physical point of origin.",
